<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Revolt of the Three Feudatories</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1673–1681</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Three Feudatories</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Revolt of the Three Feudatories was a rebellion in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> between 1673 and 1681 led by powerful Chinese generals, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-sangui">Wu Sangui</a>, who had been granted hereditary fiefdoms for helping the Qing conquest. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> moved to abolish these fiefdoms, they rose against the dynasty. Its suppression, described in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> profile, consolidated Qing control over the whole of China.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/revolt-of-the-three-feudatories.jpg" alt="Revolt of the Three Feudatories" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Three_Feudatories.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
